## T-M17 between the translation units that still declare a managed call, in
## every link mode (ImplementationPlan.md 5.20.4 MR4 and 5.20.3 T-M17 as
## amended in section 12: a unit counts when its compiled code still declares
## the managed call; milestone MC-1). A CIL object declares an external
## managed call by its stub '__msil_mc.<symbol>', and a bitcode input by the
## declaration '__msil_mc.<symbol>' that its symbol table lists; an
## !msil.managed.calls entry whose declaration the unit's own optimizer
## deleted does not count. msil-ld compares every external managed call that
## two or more units declare, forwarded or not: in object and mixed links by
## the units' stubs, then by their forwarders; between bitcode inputs by their
## entries and declarations, which must make equal stubs as in an object link,
## before LTO in every LTO mode (one module included), and two different
## entries of one name inside one input too. It reports
##   'managed call '<f>' is declared with different 'msil_method' or 'msil_catch' arguments in different translation units'
## once per name, then writes no output and runs no LTO, and slot P0 does not
## compare the entries of the modules that msil-ld's LTO compiles, so no LTO
## partition or ThinLTO module repeats the line. Inside one input, where
## msil-ld sees one declaration of a name, msil-ld also gives the line
## before LTO, once, for a direct call that the declaration's stub does not
## serve (one with another function type, or with a signext or zeroext of
## its own that would change the stub), as slot P0 gives it for clang -c and
## llc. The members are FakeWidgets.il's (managed-calls.test), on
## x86_64-pc-windows-msvc-msil; the last four items also, or only, use
## msil64-unknown-dotnet, as each says. Every link that fails counts its
## error lines.
## - s1.ll binds f to Contoso.Widget::Guarded(int32), and g calls it; s2.ll
##   binds f to Contoso.Point::Twice(int32), and k calls it only through
##   h(x, 0), whose call is dead once h is inlined: the IR at -O0, which keeps
##   the call; s2dead.ll is that unit at -O2, whose optimizer deleted the call
##   and f's declaration but kept the entry; sm.ll's main calls g and k. s1
##   with s2 fails with the line, naming f, as CIL objects in both input
##   orders, in mixed links with either unit as the object in both orders, in
##   full LTO in both orders, as ThinLTO modules (opt -module-summary; sm's
##   module imports g and k) in both orders, under --lto-partitions=2 and =3,
##   and under --lto-emit-asm in both orders. The object and mixed links used
##   to link, each unit calling its own member. s1 with s2dead links in each
##   of those modes without a diagnostic, and the image or text has one
##   binding of f, Guarded; the LTO links used to fail with the line, once
##   per partition, and from the ThinLTO module of main, which imported k and
##   with it s2dead's entry.
## - Without ThinLTO imports (s1ni.ll, s2ni.ll, s2nidead.ll and smni.ll: as
##   above, with g and k noinline, so ThinLTO imports neither into main's
##   module): the -O0 pair fails with the line as CIL objects, as ThinLTO
##   modules in both orders and in full LTO, and the -O2 pair (s2nidead.ll)
##   links in each. The -O0 objects used to link, and full LTO used to refuse
##   the -O2 pair while ThinLTO linked it.
## - One input that holds two entries of f (m12.ll, the llvm-link of s1.ll
##   and s2.ll: one declaration, s1.ll's, and both entries; m12dead.ll, the
##   llvm-link of s1.ll and s2dead.ll): msil-ld compares the entries inside
##   the input, by name, so an entry counts while its input declares the call,
##   even when the unit it came from no longer did (s2dead.ll's entry beside
##   s1.ll's live call). Beside sm.ll, each fails with the line once in full
##   LTO in both orders, as ThinLTO modules in both orders, under
##   --lto-partitions=2 and =3, and under --lto-emit-asm. The ThinLTO links
##   used to print the line twice (from both modules) and the partitioned
##   links once per partition.
## - C++ (t1.ll, t2.ll, tm.ll): a header's inline function twice() calls f,
##   and a macro picks the member, Contoso.Widget::Guarded in t1.cpp and
##   Contoso.Point::Twice in t2.cpp; g and k call twice() and f. The line
##   names the mangled symbol, '?f@@YAHH@Z', as CIL objects in both orders,
##   in mixed links in both orders, in full LTO and as ThinLTO modules. The
##   objects and mixed links used to link with both stubs, the COMDAT
##   twice() of the first object calling its own unit's member for both units.
## - The other managed-call errors print one line in a link whose LTO
##   compiles more than one module: under --lto-partitions=2, and as ThinLTO
##   modules in both orders, where main's module imports the function that
##   holds the use. T-M10,
##   'taking the address of the managed-call function '<f>' is not supported for MSIL yet',
##   for one holding function (hold.ll passes f to a callback; hmain.ll
##   passes hkeep.ll's keep, which stores it): msil-ld reports it before
##   LTO's optimizer (managed-calls-address.test), so the line names the
##   input and the function whose code there holds the use, hold.ll's input
##   and hold, in each of those links, before main inlines or imports hold.
##   T-M17 for one direct call of f with another function type (cast.ll:
##   long long through a cast of int f(int)), which msil-ld reports before
##   LTO too (managed-calls-address.test), and slot P0's invalid-attributes
##   error,
##   'managed call '<f>' has invalid "msil-managed-*" attributes: parameter <i>: conversion '<c>' does not fit the .NET type '<T>' and the IR type '<ty>'',
##   for one declaration (bad.ll: an i64 for Guarded's int32). The ThinLTO
##   links used to print each line twice (from both modules), and the
##   partitioned link the invalid-attributes line once per partition. The
##   T-M10 line used to come from slot P0 in LTO's code generation, as
##   '<unknown>:0:0: in function <h> <type>: taking the address ...', and
##   named main, which inlines hold, in the partitioned link; the T-M17
##   line came from slot P0 too.
## - A narrow result's signext against its zeroext: the stub converts the
##   member's result as the declaration extends it, conv.i1 or conv.u1
##   (conv.i2 or conv.u2), so the two units make different stubs. o8rs.ll
##   and o8.ll bind f to Contoso.Widget::Octet(uint8) as signed char
##   f(unsigned char) and as unsigned char f(unsigned char), as clang declares
##   them for msil64-unknown-dotnet: the result signext and zeroext, the
##   parameter zeroext in both; l16rs.ll and l16.ll bind f to
##   Contoso.Widget::Letter(char) with a signext and a zeroext i16 result
##   (the C types that carry CIL 'char', char16_t and a 16-bit wchar_t, are
##   never signext, so l16rs.ll is hand-written); use_a and use_b call f,
##   and nmain.ll's main calls both. Each unit is one body after the head of
##   msil64-unknown-dotnet (net.head) or of x86_64-pc-windows-msvc-msil
##   (win.head, where clang gives char and short neither attribute; the IR
##   behaves the same), and on each triple each pair fails with the line as
##   CIL objects in both orders, in mixed links with either unit as the
##   object in both orders, in full LTO, under --lto-partitions=2, as ThinLTO
##   modules and under --lto-emit-asm, each in both orders. The LTO links
##   used to link silently, both callers calling one of the two stubs: in
##   full LTO, under --lto-partitions=2 and under --lto-emit-asm the first
##   unit's, so the output depended on the input order.
## - A narrow 'val' parameter's signext against its zeroext (o8ps.ll,
##   unsigned char f(signed char) for Octet, with o8.ll): the call truncates
##   the stub's int32 argument to the member's uint8 (ECMA-335 III.1.6), so
##   the two units make equal stubs, and on each triple the pair links
##   without a diagnostic as CIL objects, in those mixed links, in full LTO,
##   under --lto-partitions=2, as ThinLTO modules and under --lto-emit-asm,
##   each in both orders, and the first stub of each image or text calls
##   Octet and ends with conv.u1.
## - One input that holds both units of a pair, as llvm-link merges them:
##   the first unit's declaration with its attributes, each unit's call with
##   its own, and both units' entries, which are equal. msil-ld gives the
##   line before LTO, as slot P0 gives it for clang -c and llc, for a direct
##   call with the declaration's function type whose own signext or zeroext,
##   which the declaration does not have there, would change the
##   declaration's stub (ImplementationPlan.md section 12, 2026-11-11), the
##   calls in available_externally functions aside. o8m.ll (o8rs.ll and
##   o8.ll: the result signext in the declaration and zeroext in use_b's
##   call) and l16m.ll (l16.ll and l16rs.ll: zeroext in the declaration and
##   signext in use_a's call) each fail beside nmain.ll on each triple, with
##   the line once, in full LTO and as ThinLTO modules in both orders, under
##   --lto-partitions=2 and under --lto-emit-asm, and write no output; they
##   used to link without a diagnostic, both callers calling the
##   declaration's stub (conv.i1 for o8m.ll's use_b, conv.u2 for l16m.ll's
##   use_a). e8m.ll merges e8s.ll and e8.ll, which bind f to
##   Contoso.Widget::SetMode(valuetype Contoso.Mode), an int32-backed enum,
##   as void f(signed char) and as void f(unsigned char), as clang declares
##   them for msil64-unknown-dotnet: an 'enum' parameter's IL type in the
##   stub follows its zeroext, int8 or uint8, so the two units make
##   different stubs, and on msil64-unknown-dotnet they fail with the line
##   as separate inputs in every mode of the result pairs. With --reference,
##   e8m.ll fails on each triple with the line alone, in full LTO and under
##   --lto-emit-asm, before LTO, so neither link's checks of the stub's
##   types run; both links used to fail with T-M7 instead,
##   'managed call 'f': parameter 1 passes 8 bits for 'Contoso.Mode', which is not an enum of that width'.
## - One input whose calls differ from the declaration only where the stub
##   does not change links on each triple without a diagnostic, in those LTO
##   modes, with one stub: o8pm.ll (o8ps.ll and o8.ll: the 'val' parameter
##   signext in the declaration and zeroext in use_b's call), whose stub
##   calls Octet and ends with conv.u1, and b8m.ll (b8rs.ll and b8.ll bind f
##   to Contoso.Widget::Check(bool) as signed char f(unsigned char) and as
##   unsigned char f(unsigned char): the stub makes a 'bool' result 0 or 1
##   whatever its extension, so the two units make equal stubs, and on
##   msil64-unknown-dotnet they link as separate inputs as the 'val'
##   parameter pair does), whose stub calls Check and ends with ldc.i4.0 and
##   cgt.un.

# REQUIRES: llvm-cil-dump
# RUN: rm -rf %t && split-file %s %t && cd %t
# RUN: mkdir refs
# RUN: yaml2obj %S/Inputs/fakepack/FakeRuntime.yaml -o refs/FakeRuntime.dll
# RUN: yaml2obj %S/Inputs/fakepack/FakeWidgets.yaml -o refs/FakeWidgets.dll
# DEFINE: %{crash} = --implicit-check-not="PLEASE submit" --implicit-check-not="Stack dump" \
# DEFINE:   --implicit-check-not="LLVM ERROR"
# DEFINE: %{err} = FileCheck %s %{crash} --implicit-check-not=error: --implicit-check-not=warning:
## A failed link's error lines (err.txt), counted, then each pinned whole.
# DEFINE: %{one} = grep error: err.txt | count 1 && %{err} --input-file=err.txt
## A linked image whose one binding of f is Guarded.
# DEFINE: %{guarded} = FileCheck %s --check-prefix=GUARDED --implicit-check-not=Twice

# TM17:    {{^}}msil-ld: error: managed call 'f' is declared with different 'msil_method' or 'msil_catch' arguments in different translation units{{$}}
# GUARDED: {{^  }}.method '__msil_mc.f' int32(int32) {{.+$}}
# GUARDED: {{^    L[0-9]+: }}call int32(int32) [FakeWidgets]Contoso.Widget::'Guarded'{{$}}

## The units as CIL objects, bitcode and ThinLTO modules.
